---
/**
 * DraftBlock — what his pipeline wrote about a saved piece before he read it.
 *
 * **This is the one block on the site that is not him**, and every decision in
 * it follows from that. `lib/library.ts › Draft` is a separate type from
 * `Digest` for the same reason: a digest is a judgement somebody committed to
 * and a draft is a placeholder holding the page open, and folding the two
 * together would let the site render an unearned opinion in his voice.
 *
 * Three things keep the two apart where a reader can see it.
 *
 *   - **It is boxed.** Everything else down a `/library/<slug>` page is prose
 *     under a mono label, flush left on the page background. This is the only
 *     thing inside a border, which is what says it is quoted rather than said.
 *   - **The label is at full ink**, against the `--faint` every other label on
 *     an entry page takes. A label whose whole job is to stop a reader taking
 *     the paragraph under it as his verdict cannot be the quietest thing in the
 *     box.
 *   - **The body is third person and stays third person.** The pipeline writes
 *     about the piece, never as him. The moment he writes his own, it moves to
 *     the entry's top-level `why` and renders through `VoiceBlocks` with every
 *     other sentence he has written on this site. That is a field move, not a
 *     relabel, so no rendering bug can turn one register into the other.
 *
 * The labels are spelled here rather than in `lib/markdown.ts › DIGEST_LABELS`.
 * That list exists because a digest is rendered twice — as blocks here and as
 * `###` sections in `/library.md` — and a label with two renderings is a label
 * that can drift. A draft has one rendering, so a shared const would be
 * indirection bought for nothing. It moves the day `/library.md` grows a
 * drafted section, and `lib/detail.test.mjs` is what holds the words in place
 * until then.
 */
import type { Draft } from "../lib/library";

interface Props {
  draft: Draft;
}

const { draft } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="draft">
  <h2 class="draft__label mono">Drafted, not read</h2>

  {/*
    His sentence about somebody else's words, which is the one bit of this box
    in his voice. It says who wrote the thing and what to do with it, and then
    it stops: a paragraph explaining how the pipeline works would be the page
    showing its work (design.md §6).
  */}
  <p class="draft__frame">
    My pipeline wrote this from the piece itself. I haven’t read it yet, so take
    it as a summary and not a call.
  </p>

  {
    draft.bullets && (
      <ul class="draft__bullets">
        {draft.bullets.map((bullet) => (
          <li class="draft__bullet">{bullet}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    )
  }

  {draft.why && <p class="draft__body">{draft.why}</p>}

  <p class="draft__date mono tabular-nums">Drafted {draft.drafted}</p>
</section>

<style>
  /* Structural, so sharp: design.md §3. A radius here would make the box a
     token you could pick up rather than a surface holding somebody else's
     words. `--hairline-strong` rather than `--hairline`, because every other
     border on a page of prose is the quiet one and this box exists to be
     noticed. */
  .draft {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 8vh, 4rem);
    padding: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 1.35rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline-strong);
    border-radius: 0;
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  /* Full ink, against the `--faint` a digest label takes. The whole job of this
     word is to stop a reader taking the box as his verdict. */
  .draft__label {
    color: var(--fg);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .draft__frame {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* A real list, marked as one, the same shape `DigestBlocks.astro` gives the
     cliff notes: these are claims pulled out of the piece. */
  .draft__bullets {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    list-style: disc;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.55rem;
  }

  .draft__bullet,
  .draft__body {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .draft__bullet::marker {
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .draft__body {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* The label's own date, at the foot rather than beside the heading: it is the
     day this was written and not part of the warning. `--faint` is right here,
     which is the layer static metadata sits on everywhere else (design.md §4). */
  .draft__date {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--faint);
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 599px) {
    .draft {
      max-width: none;
    }
  }
</style>
